# Syntax: docs/LANGUAGE.md. Check with `rbb bid check`.
#
# A major-suit fit, whatever the path (Rick, 2026-10-02): suit agreement,
# which both hands know, and a fit that only one hand can see yet. The
# conditions are named here for every module to read; the rules below are
# the judgment for the second: the hand that sees the fit places the
# contract in it. fit.notes.md has the ruling, the survey and the
# measurements.

module fit "A major-suit fit: agreed, or seen by one hand"
  needs  base

# ── The two concepts ──────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# Suit agreement (public): what each hand has shown makes eight cards
# (1NT-4D Texas: two facing six), or a rule has set the trump suit. Both
# hands know it, so it makes x trump: 4NT asks for keycards in x, control
# bids refer to x.
define agreed_fit(x) = trump is x | shown.x.min + partner.x.min >= 8

# A private fit: my own length and what partner has shown make eight,
# what I have shown does not (1NT-2D-2H with six hearts; a doubleton
# facing opener's six-card rebid). Partner does not know it yet, so it
# changes nothing about what 4NT means; it tells me where to play.
define private_fit(x) = x + partner.x.min >= 8, shown.x.min + partner.x.min <= 7

# ── The count, by role (Rick, 2026-10-01) ─────────────────────────────
#
# The long-trump hand counts declarer points (HCP + length), the short
# hand support points (HCP + shortness, capped by its trumps), each on
# partner's floor (`longer`, slam-entry.bid). Game at 25.
define fit_game_values(t)   = (longer(t), hcp + length_points + partner.points.min >= 25) | (!longer(t), tp(t) + partner.points.min >= 25)

# A slam call the slam modules would make in the fit instead: the direct
# slam on a card with no ask, the keycard ask agreeing it on one with
# (slam-entry.bid, rkcb-1430.bid, blackwood.bid).
define fit_slam_call(t) = (no_ask, direct_count(t)) | (!no_ask, shown_fit(t), fit_slam_values(t), !two_bare_beside(t))

# In a game force, a fit in partner's own suit (three or more shown) with
# slam interest is agreed slowly (slam-entry.bid, "Fast arrival"): the
# jump to game would deny the interest.
define fit_slow_agree(t) = we.forcing = game, partner.t.min >= 3, under_game(t), t + partner.t.min >= 8, slam_interest(t)
define slam_route = fit_slow_agree(S) | fit_slow_agree(H) | fit_slam_call(S) | fit_slam_call(H)

# Slam interest in the fit below 3NT leaves room for the forcing calls
# that explore it (new minor forcing, a jump shift, a raise below game);
# over partner's 3NT, slam values in notrump (32 HCP on his floor, the
# quantitative 4NT and 6NT of over-3nt.bid) go there.
define fit_slam_room(t) = (slam_interest(t), !partner.last=3N) | (partner.last=3N, hcp + partner.hcp.min >= 32)

# Four cards in the other major, which partner may hold four of: look for
# that fit first (1D-1S-1NT-3H with six spades and four hearts).
define other_major_open(x) = x >= 4, partner.x.max >= 4

# Partner's last bid was in x: raising it is the sequence modules' job
# (a Stayman answer, opener's suit), and their raises say more.
define partner_named(x) = partner.last=1{x} | partner.last=2{x} | partner.last=3{x} | partner.last=4{x}

# ── Placing the contract in a fit only I can see ──────────────────────
#
# Rick (2026-10-02): the hand that knows the fit places the contract in
# it, by strength, rather than in notrump. Game in the fit with 25
# between us (`fit_game_values`), ahead of the notrump calls at the same
# point (priority 1: the sequence rules are at 0). Bidding the suit this
# way shows the fit, so partner knows it too.
#
# Where, measured (fit.notes.md): at my second call or later, with
# nothing asked of me and no suit agreed, uncontested or with the
# opponents silent since (RHO passed: 1S X P 2S P 3NT P), when partner's
# last call was notrump (his 1NT, 2NT or 3NT rebid: balanced, so two or
# more in the major; over his 3NT, four of the major is the correction;
# his notrump answer to a question of mine too: a stopper ask, the
# fourth suit), or when he has answered my new minor forcing or Stayman
# in a suit. Elsewhere a
# priority-1 4M would claim how partner reads the sequence modules' own
# 4M (a jump raise's range: slam-entry.test), and an invitation at the
# three level would claim their forcing three-level bids, so neither is
# written. Not when partner's last bid was the suit (raising it is the
# sequence modules' job), not over the strong 2NT (transfers may be on),
# not when a slam call or slam exploration applies (`slam_route`,
# `fit_slam_room`), and not with four cards in the other major that
# partner may share (`other_major_open`).
#
# `private_fit(x)` is the rule's condition written out: the hand part in
# `shows`, so that partner reads my length from it, the public part in
# `when`. `partner.x.min >= 2` keeps the rule from claiming partner's
# reading of a call it never made (1S-1NT-3H, a jump shift).
when !asked, we.trump is none, !game_reached | partner.last=3N, !they.bid | rho.last=P, me.has_bid, !(partner.last=2N, partner.hcp.min>=20), partner.last=1N | partner.last=2N | partner.last=3N | answered nmf | answered majors
  4S  "Game in the spade fit: my length and partner's make eight"
      shows S + partner.S.min >= 8, fit_game_values(S)
      when  shown.S.min + partner.S.min <= 7, partner.S.min >= 2, !partner_named(S), !slam_route, !fit_slam_room(S), !other_major_open(H)
      priority 1
  4H  "Game in the heart fit: my length and partner's make eight"
      shows H + partner.H.min >= 8, fit_game_values(H)
      when  shown.H.min + partner.H.min <= 7, partner.H.min >= 2, !partner_named(H), !slam_route, !fit_slam_room(H), !other_major_open(S)
      priority 1

